WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:03.740
Let's go next to our caller from the six zero
nine area code.

00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:06.029
Who's calling today from six zero nine.

00:00:06.029 --> 00:00:09.740
Hey, David, this is Boaz from New Jersey.

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:11.640
Hey Boaz, what's up?

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:16.839
Uh, so I just wanted to, uh, ask you a question.

00:00:16.839 --> 00:00:21.720
How long do you think it's going to take and,
or what is it going to take to completely

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:29.079
flip the democratic party to be a better representative
or a middle working class people?

00:00:29.079 --> 00:00:34.930
Um, we elected AOC, um, representative Ilhan
Omar.

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:38.380
Um, and let's say we elected Bernie Sanders.

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:41.430
Um, and let's say this are two terms.

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:44.320
Um, which would be great.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:52.200
What is it going to take to create a progressive
party and make that the democratic party?

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:53.200
What do you think?

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:57.930
Well, what you have to understand is that
if it were just a matter of the country being

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:03.420
more progressive, we would have had a far
more progressive democratic party years ago.

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:08.289
And that's where you have to integrate into
the analysis, the effect of money in politics

00:01:08.289 --> 00:01:14.920
and the disproportionate outsized influence
of big corporations and money in politics.

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:19.380
Because the population has been well to the
left of the democratic party for a long time.

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:24.979
When you poll people about healthcare, when
you pull people about, uh, abortion, gay marriage,

00:01:24.979 --> 00:01:30.020
the war on drugs, drug legalization, even
on money in politics itself, the American

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:33.439
public is well to the left of where our elected
officials are.

00:01:33.439 --> 00:01:38.070
And so what you have to then understand is
that even though the democratic party is certainly

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:44.969
to the left of the Republican party, because
the same corporate influences are affecting

00:01:44.969 --> 00:01:50.759
both parties to similar degrees, that is really
what is holding back the democratic party

00:01:50.759 --> 00:01:51.759
is simply moving to the left.

00:01:51.759 --> 00:01:52.759
There.

00:01:52.759 --> 00:01:53.759
There's a whole bunch of different reforms.

00:01:53.759 --> 00:01:57.939
There's no doubt that if Bernie were to have
eight years and the house continues moving

00:01:57.939 --> 00:02:02.579
more to the left and democratic senators are
replaced with more progressive democratic

00:02:02.579 --> 00:02:06.310
senators, of course that's going to move the
democratic party to the left.

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:11.549
But to really break the status quo, you've
got to fix campaign finance.

00:02:11.549 --> 00:02:16.670
You've got to potentially put in place term
limits, which are also a factor, uh, that

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:20.510
that prevents, um, our elected officials from
just doing what the people want rather than

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:24.030
figuring out, well, what are corporate interests
going to do when I'm up for reelection if

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:25.290
I do this or I do that.

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:29.510
So there's a whole bunch of systemic change
that needs to happen in addition to continuing

00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:31.720
to elect people that are further to the left.

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:32.720
Okay.

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:34.780
Thank you very much.

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:36.400
All right, Boaz from New Jersey.

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:37.389
Great to hear from you.

